 B is the set of all the elements in A but not in B,\n{𝑥|𝑥 ∈ 𝐴𝑎𝑛𝑑𝑥 ∉ 𝐵}, denoted as 𝐴\\𝐵 ( 𝐴 − 𝐵) or 𝐴~𝐵)\nWhat does the Venn diagram of 𝐴\\𝐵 look like?\nA B\n𝐴\\𝐵 is shaded\n14\nExamples:\na. Let B = {2, 3, 6, 10, 13, 15} and A = {2, 10, 15, 21, 22}.\nThen B\\A =B – A = {3, 6, 13}.\nb. Let X be the set of natural numbers and A be the set of odd natural numbers. Then,\nX – A = the set of even natural numbers; or X – A = {x | x is a natural number and x is even}.\n1.6.4 The Symmetric difference of sets A and B\ndenoted by 𝐴 ⊕ 𝐵, consists of those elements which belongs to A or B but not to both. That is,\n𝐴 ⊕ 𝐵 = (𝐴 ∪ 𝐵)(𝐴 ∩ 𝐵) or 𝐴 ⊕ 𝐵 = {𝑥 | 𝑥 ∈ 𝐴, 𝑥 ∈ 𝐵, 𝑥 ∉ 𝐴 ∩ 𝐵} or\n𝐴 ⊕ 𝐵 = (𝐴\\𝐵) ∪ (𝐵\\𝐴)\nWhat does the Venn diagram of this look like?\nA B\n𝐴 ⊕ 𝐵is shaded\nExample\nSuppose U = N = {1, 2, 3, 4, ….} is the universal set.\nLet A = {1, 2, 3, 4}, B = {3, 4, 5, 6, 7}, C = {2, 3, 8, 9}, E = {2, 4, 6, 8, ….}\nThen:\n𝑨 ⊕ 𝑩 = {𝟏, 𝟐, 𝟓, 𝟔, 𝟕}, 𝑩 ⊕ 𝑪 = {𝟐, 𝟒, 𝟓, 𝟔, 𝟕, 𝟖, 𝟗}\n𝑨 ⊕ 𝑪 = {𝟏, 𝟒, 𝟖, 𝟗}, 𝑨 ⊕ 𝑬 = {𝟏, 𝟑, 𝟔, 𝟖, 𝟏𝟎, . . . . . }\nThe operations ∪,∩ and\\can be repeated or combined to derive more sets.\nExample\nLet 𝐴 = {1,2,3,4}, 𝐵 = {3,4,5,6,7} and 𝐶 = {6,7,8}.\n. Express in tabular form the composite sets:\ni. (𝐴 ∪ 𝐵) ∩ 𝐶 = {1,2,3,4, ⥂ 5,6,7} ∩ {6,7,8} = {6,7}.\nii. (𝐴 ∪ 𝐶)(𝐵 ∪ 𝐶) = {1,2}\nNote that in any mathematical expression containing brackets ( ), we must perform the\noperations inside the bracket(s) first\nOther examples will be discussed in the lecture\n15\n1.6.5 The disjoint of two sets\nA and B are said to be disjoint or nonintersecting if they have no elements in common or, using\nthe definition of intersection, 𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 = 𝜑, the empty set.\nSuppose 𝑆 = 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵 and 𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 = 𝜑 , then S is called the disjoint union of A and B.\nExample\nLet 𝐴 = {1,2,3,4}, 𝐵 = {3,4,5,6,7} and 𝐶 = {6,7,8}.\nThen 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵 = {1,2,3,4,5,6,7},\n𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 = {3,4},\n𝐶\\𝐵 = {8}.\nNote that A and C are disjoint (i.e. their intersection is the empty set).\nThe following properties of union and intersection should be noted.\nProperty 1: Every element 𝑥 in 𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 belongs to both A and B; hence 𝑥 belongs to 𝐴 and\n𝑥 belongs to B. Thus 𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 is a subset of A and of B; namely\n𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 ⊆ 𝐴 and 𝐴 ∩ 𝐵 ⊆ 𝐵\nProperty 2: An element 𝑥 belongs to the union 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵 if 𝑥 belongs to 𝐴 or 𝑥 belongs to 𝐵\nHence every element in 𝐴 belongs to 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵, and every element in 𝐵 belongs to 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵.\nThat is, 𝐴 ⊆ 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵 and 𝐵 ⊆ 𝐴 ∪ 𝐵.\n1.6.6 Duality:\nLet 𝐸 be an equation of set algebra. The dual 𝑬∗of 𝐸 is the equation obtained by replacing each\noccurrence of ⋃, ⋂, U, Ø in E by ⋂, ⋃, Ø, U respectively.\nExample: (U⋂ A) ⋃ (B⋂ A) =A is (Ø⋃ A ) ⋂ (B⋃ A) = A.\n16","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"uDVjP3paN7"}],"key":"fOFW1C5O3s"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition 1.? - Symmetric difference","position":{"start":{"line":98,"column":1},"end":{"line":98,"column":1}},"key":"hMzajFXdd5"}],"key":"XPkUAX21ex"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"key":"yUNk41Xjj2"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"symmetric difference of two sets","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"key":"zfnw90ipxu"}],"key":"DhmhCyBFOx"},{"type":"text","value":" ","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"key":"Zxkss70LBJ"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"A","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":101,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" a